Evidence: `src/features/plugins/fleet/fleetTerminalManager.ts`. The registry keeps one xterm per session; `parked: string[]` (globalThis-persisted) records detach order and `MAX_PARKED = 6` bounds it:

```ts
// Cycling focus through a 40-session fleet must not accumulate 40 terminals:
// beyond MAX_PARKED the oldest parked one is disposed. Lossless in practice —
// a re-attach re-subscribes and replays the backend ring snapshot anyway
while (parked.length > MAX_PARKED) {
  const oldest = parked[0]!;
  if (registry.get(oldest)?.attached) { parked.shift(); continue; }
  disposeTerminal(oldest);
}
```

The eviction loop also guards against inconsistent entries (an attached session in the parked list is skipped, not disposed). The key precondition documented in the header comment: each xterm holds a 5000-line scrollback, so the bound is what lets a 40-session fleet cycle focus without accumulating 40 idle instances. This refines the existing 'Own expensive instances in a registry — detach is not dispose' canon: the registry alone is a leak; the LRU + replay-backed eviction closes it.

Evidence: `src/features/plugins/fleet/fleetTerminalManager.ts::ensureSharedOutputListener`:

```ts
if (g[OUTPUT_LISTENER_KEY]) return;
g[OUTPUT_LISTENER_KEY] = true; // set eagerly so a re-entrant call can't double-listen
listen(EventName.FLEET_SESSION_OUTPUT, (event) => {
  const m = registry.get(event.payload.session_id);
  if (!m) return; // backend only emits for subscribed sessions; unknown ids drop
  ...
})
  .then((fn) => { g[OUTPUT_LISTENER_KEY] = fn; })
  .catch((e) => { g[OUTPUT_LISTENER_KEY] = undefined; /* allow retry */ ... });
```

The header documents the anti-pattern it replaced: "The previous design registered one filtered listener per terminal, so every output chunk ran N callbacks with N terminals ever-created." The flag lives on `globalThis` so HMR doesn't stack a second listener. Three separable defenses: (1) one dispatch map, (2) eager flag against the listen-in-flight race, (3) flag reset on rejection so failure is not permanent.

Evidence: `src/features/plugins/fleet/FleetTerminalOverlay.tsx` carries the post-mortem as a NOTE:

```
// NOTE: this component must NEVER write `fleetGridOpen`. That flag is now
// the *input* that decides whether the app-wide `FleetGridLayer` mounts this
// overlay at all — it used to also be an output here (an effect that set it
// on mount and cleared it on unmount ...). Once the layer started rendering
// FROM the flag that became a cycle: under StrictMode's mount → cleanup →
// mount effect double-invoke, the cleanup's `setGridOpen(false)` re-entered
// the layer's render mid-flush and left the overlay wedged open.
```

The original write existed so the Athena orb could lift itself above the overlay — but the orb reads the same store flag, so deleting the write lost nothing. The distilled rule: when refactoring a self-managing overlay into a flag-mounted layer, audit every effect that wrote the flag; each one is now a potential mount cycle.

Evidence: `src/features/plugins/fleet/FleetTerminalOverlay.tsx`:

```ts
// Two-phase open (perf): paint the overlay frame + tile chrome/status blocks
// on the FIRST frame, and only mount the expensive tile bodies (xterm attach,
// WebGL context creation) a beat later. Opening the grid used to pay the
// whole xterm cost inside the opening render, which is what made the switch feel heavy.
const [bodiesReady, setBodiesReady] = useState(false);
useEffect(() => {
  if (!open) return;
  const id = window.setTimeout(() => setBodiesReady(true), 200);
  return () => window.clearTimeout(id);
}, [open]);
```

`bodiesReady` is ANDed into each tile's `live` prop, so every tile renders its status-block skeleton immediately and upgrades in place. Distinct from lazy chunk loading (the module is already loaded — `FleetGridLayer` lazy-imports the host separately); this staggers instantiation cost, not download cost.

Evidence: `src/features/plugins/fleet/fleetSessionScope.ts` — the whole module is the contract:

```ts
return [...sessions].sort(
  (a, b) => Number(a.createdAtMs) - Number(b.createdAtMs) || a.id.localeCompare(b.id),
);
```

The tie-break defends against a real nondeterminism: "the Rust snapshot iterates a HashMap — without the tie-break, ties would re-shuffle per fetch." Wake keeps the slot by inheriting `createdAtMs` (`registry::adopt_lineage` on the Rust side). `useFleetOverlayActions.ts::handleKill` implements the state-aware trash (live → `killSession`, tombstone → `removeSession`), and `FleetGridLayer.tsx` only auto-minimizes the overlay when NOTHING is tracked ("tiles never vanish out from under the operator"). Motivation recorded from the first live run: any membership change compacts a CSS grid, breaking 'session X is the top-left tile' muscle memory. Reusable wherever operators build spatial memory over parallel work (terminals, dashboards, kanban swimlanes).

Evidence: `src/features/fleet/monitor/useMonitorData.ts::useInFlight` + `verdictKey`:

```ts
/** In-flight key for one verdict on one row. Two calls join ONLY when they ask
 *  the backend for the same thing. */
function verdictKey(id: string, intent: string): string {
  return `review:${id}:${intent}`;
}
```

The doc comment records both prior failure modes: the original single `if (isProcessing) return;` dropped a reviewer's second verdict on the floor at one-card-per-second triage speed; the next iteration keyed `review:${id}` for both writers, so "a *different* verdict on the same row issued inside one round-trip silently inherited the first one's outcome and reported success." The principle generalizes: joining is only honest between calls that ask for the same thing; dedup keys must encode what the call asks for, not merely what it touches. Dispatch actions get their own intent namespace (`action:${action}`).

Evidence: `src/features/plugins/fleet/fleetGridView.ts`:

```ts
export function peekGridViewOnOpen(sessionCount: number): GridViewId {
  if (requestedView) return requestedView;
  if (explicitPick) return lastGridView;
  return sessionCount > MONITOR_DEFAULT_ABOVE ? 'monitor' : 'tiles';
}
export function resolveGridViewOnOpen(sessionCount: number): GridViewId {
  const view = peekGridViewOnOpen(sessionCount);
  requestedView = null; // consume
  return view;
}
```

Consumed in `FleetTerminalOverlay.tsx`: `useState(() => peekGridViewOnOpen(...))` for the initializer (render must stay pure) and `resolveGridViewOnOpen` in the open effect. Two guard rails in the same component: the landing view re-resolves on every OPEN, not on every session change ("the view must not swap out from under the operator while they are reading it" — session count is read through a ref), and the size default flips to the dense ledger past 12 live sessions because tiles smaller than a useful terminal are the wrong first read.

Evidence: `src/features/fleet/monitor/channels/lensModel.ts::facetCounts`:

```ts
const without = (patch: Partial<LensState>) => {
  const l = { ...lens, ...patch };
  return rows.filter((r) => matchesLens(r, l, nameOf));
};
const kindRows = without({ kinds: new Set() }); // clear ONLY this dimension
```

Each dimension re-filters with itself cleared, then tallies its own values over the survivors. The Stream's header comment states the intent: "Each count is computed against the rows surviving the OTHER dimensions, so '17' means 'selecting this shows 17 rows', not a misleading global total." Supporting detail: `matchesLens` deliberately re-checks the server-enforced kind dimension too, making it "a total predicate — safe to run over a cache that was fetched blended"; and callsign facets skip Slack rows whose `personaId` is actually a Slack user id that would render as a nameless SYSTEM entry.

Evidence: `src/features/plugins/fleet/useFleetHotkeys.ts`:

```ts
if (e.ctrlKey || e.metaKey || e.altKey) return;
const target = e.target as HTMLElement | null;
if (target?.closest('input, textarea, select, [contenteditable="true"]')) return;
```

The doc comment names the non-obvious case: the contenteditable check "also covers xterm.js's hidden helper textarea, so keys typed INTO a focused terminal never trigger fleet navigation" — a real hazard in any app embedding a terminal or rich editor. `gridOpen` narrows the set to `g`/`n` ("arrow focus-moves and search make no sense over the overlay"), all handlers are optional ("an absent handler simply makes its key a no-op"), and `handlersRef.current = handlers` keeps the listener stable across renders.

Evidence: `src/features/fleet/monitor/live/LiveChannelOverlay.tsx::LiveFeedSink`:

```ts
const isLive = established.current || (Number.isFinite(atMs) && atMs >= mountAt.current - NEW_GRACE_MS);
...
established.current = true;
if (seen.current.size > 800) seen.current = new Set(merged.map((m) => m.item.id));
```

`NEW_GRACE_MS = 8000` keeps a message that arrived just before mount from being swallowed as history. The queue engine caps accumulation at 30 (`CAP`), expires by TTL with hover-pause time subtracted (`pausedTotal` per id), and the disable effect clears incoming/dismissed/pause maps. Also a routing decision worth copying for agent UIs: the resident companion's rows never pop here — "Athena speaks on exactly two dimensions: the chat window and the orb. A corner pop-up is a third one" — channel presence per author kind is an explicit policy, not an accident of the feed.

Evidence: `src/features/fleet/monitor/monitorModel.ts::buildMonitorModel`:

```ts
// A display label is not a namespaced key: two personas can share a name, and
// a system/app process's free-form label can coincidentally equal a persona's
// name. Track collisions so the fallback below only fires when the name
// unambiguously identifies exactly one persona.
const nameToId = new Map<string, string>();
const nameCollisions = new Set<string>();
...
} else if (proc.label && nameToId.has(proc.label) && !nameCollisions.has(proc.label)) {
  owner = nameToId.get(proc.label)!;
}
```

Attribution tries three signals in confidence order: `proc.personaId` (validated against the live id set), `proc.navigateTo?.personaId`, then the collision-guarded label. Whatever fails all three lands in `systemProcesses` — visible, just unattributed. This refines the existing 'never link by scanning display strings' canon for the case where a name fallback is genuinely needed: the collision set is what makes it safe.

Evidence: `src/features/plugins/fleet/fleetAttention.ts::craftStalePrompt`. For a never-attached session (typed verdict from the Rust ticker, detected via `isNeverAttached` — a prefix match against the ticker's `state_reason` with a keep-in-sync comment pointing at `stale.rs`):

```ts
// Never-attached sessions have no live agent — don't ask Athena to "unblock"
// them (she can only decline). Frame it as the diagnosis it is so she
// confirms the kill instead of re-investigating from scratch.
return `Fleet session "${label}" ... never attached a Claude agent ... Do NOT propose fleet_send_input; there's nothing live to type into. Confirm in one line that it should be killed ...`;
```

The genuinely-ambiguous branch asks for assessment with a concrete escape hatch ("If it needs human judgment or is actually finished, say so instead of proposing an action") and pins output language. The companion request is tagged with an origin ('Fleet') so it persists as a System turn, "never an impersonated user message" (`useFleetOverlayActions.ts::handleAskAthena`).

Evidence: `src/features/plugins/fleet/FleetTerminalOverlay.tsx`, both traps documented at the render site:

```
// Portaled to `document.body` so it sits above the (transform-using)
// framer-motion layout ancestors — a plain `fixed inset-0` nested in the page
// would be positioned relative to a transformed ancestor, not the viewport.
...
// The footer gap is reserved in layout, not left to z-order. Overlapping
// and relying on the footer's higher z-index is what failed before: the
// footer lives under a `contain: layout` ancestor, so its z-index was
// scoped to that subtree and this portal painted straight over it.
```

The overlay is `top-12` (titlebar stays usable for window controls + global Back) with `style={{ bottom: DESKTOP_FOOTER_HEIGHT_PX }}` — the height constant imported from the footer component so the reservation cannot drift. It also routes the global Back button and Escape to minimize via a store-level back-interceptor, instead of letting navigation change the page underneath the overlay.

Evidence: `src-tauri/src/commands/design/template_adopt.rs`. Recipe-ref hydration during template adoption mutates `use_cases[]` in place; the code makes it fatal with a comment explaining the damage ("a green 'adopted' modal over a persona with missing capabilities"):

```rust
// Hydration is FATAL, not best-effort. `hydrate_recipe_refs` mutates
// `use_cases` IN PLACE per entry, so a mid-list failure ... leaves earlier
// UCs hydrated and later ones as bare `{recipe_ref: …}` stubs.
crate::engine::template_v3::hydrate_recipe_refs(&mut design, lookup).map_err(...)?;
// Defense in depth: even if hydration returned Ok, assert no `recipe_ref`
// stubs survive in `use_cases` before normalization.
if let Some(stub_id) = first_unhydrated_recipe_ref(&design) {
    return Err(AppError::Validation(format!(
        "... still references un-hydrated recipe '{stub_id}' after hydration — refusing to adopt a partially-configured persona.")));
}
```

`first_unhydrated_recipe_ref` (same file, ~line 810) is the post-condition scan. The failure mode it prevents is generic: any pipeline whose normalizer tolerates the pre-transform shape will silently accept a half-transformed payload.

Evidence: `src-tauri/src/commands/design/template_adopt.rs::instant_adopt_template_inner` doc comment:

```rust
