Each kind carries a `remediation()` one-liner, and `remediation_for(kind, connector)` substitutes the connector's real `login_cmd` for `CliLogin` because "a generic 'run its login command' is useless — the whole point of the kind is that a single copy-pasteable command fixes it." The module is itself the unification of two previously divergent checks (adopt vs promote) that "used to disagree, so a persona could pass adoption and then fail promote."

Evidence: `src-tauri/src/commands/design/connector_readiness.rs` module header:

```rust
//! 1. A uniquely bound, usable Vault credential → `Ready`. This ALWAYS wins:
//!    it is the only mode that survives leaving this machine (CI, another
//!    device, a headless run).
//! 2. No credential resolves → probe the provider CLI (cached, ~5 min TTL):
//!    - exit 0 (authenticated) → `Ready`
//!    - binary present, probe fails → `NeedsSetup { CliLogin }`
//!    - binary absent → `NeedsSetup { VaultCredential }`
```

Constants document the reasoning: `CLI_PROBE_TIMEOUT: 4s` ("must never hang on a CLI waiting at an interactive prompt"), `CLI_PROBE_TTL: 300s` ("long enough that the many readiness recomputes in a single user action all hit the cache; short enough that a `vercel login` the user just ran is picked up without restarting the app"). Full rationale doc referenced at `docs/architecture/connector-classification.md`.

Evidence: `src-tauri/src/commands/design/connector_readiness.rs`:

```rust
/// Whether the stderr of a failed probe says the program itself is missing.
/// On Windows the probe goes through `cmd /C`, which always spawns
/// successfully — so the "binary absent" signal has to be read out of the
/// shell's own error text (or its 9009 exit code) rather than a spawn error.
fn stderr_means_absent(stderr: &str) -> bool { ... }

// stdin closed so a CLI that would prompt fails fast instead of blocking;
// stdout discarded (we only need the exit code); stderr kept for the
